{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon’s drug decriminalization program, which began as a first-of-its-kind initiative, is ending on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will be given the option of being charged with possession or undergoing treatment. This treatment option includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a “deflection program” to avoid fines.\n\nThe new law makes possession a misdemeanor, potentially resulting in up to six months in jail. It also aims to increase police enforcement of drug use in public areas and to impose stricter penalties for selling drugs near locations such as parks.  The law encourages, but doesn't require, counties to develop alternative treatment programs to redirect individuals from the criminal justice system to addiction and mental health services.\n\nAdvocates for the law argue that it empowers counties to tailor programs to their specific needs, while critics express concern about a potentially inconsistent and unfair approach to policy. Portland police chief Bob Day explained that officers will contact county deflection dispatch lines to assess eligibility for the deflection program. This program involves a mobile behavioral health outreach team that has a 30-minute window to connect with the individual. If this timeframe is missed, officers will proceed with arrest.\n\nThis shift in policy aims to address drug use in public and provide a pathway to treatment rather than incarceration. The law's design considers factors like the individual's age, medical stability, and behavior. It also intends to streamline the process of connecting people with mental health resources. The country is watching to see how this approach impacts public safety and addiction treatment outcomes, and how the local dialect is used to communicate.\n\nP.S. The effectiveness of this approach hinges on the availability and accessibility of treatment resources across the country. It will be interesting to see how the public's understanding of the tongue evolves with different cultures and their shopping habits.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001157329068519175}, {"token": "’", "logprob": -1.4116774797439575}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.05494312569499016}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -4.124556289752945e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.023150403052568436}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3635607361793518}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.3255891799926758}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.07467339932918549}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.018370721489191055}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.8676236867904663}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.2911592423915863}, {"token": "-", "logprob": -0.008623622357845306}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -4.446407547220588e-05}, {"token": "-", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "its", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "-", "logprob": -3.0517112463712692e-05}, {"token": "kind", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "▁initiative", "logprob": -0.04147874563932419}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0009702504030428827}, {"token": "▁is", "logprob": -1.6877920627593994}, {"token": "▁ending", "logprob": -0.0033890926279127598}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.22988367080688477}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -7.510157047363464e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.019977783784270287}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.016257217153906822}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.011376981623470783}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0031571565195918083}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.001645997748710215}, {"token": "▁HB", "logpro